WEBVTT

NOTE Captions for "Binary transparency: payload, signed, and exe" (Topic 10). No
NOTE audio track yet by decision; these captions carry the narration. Source of
NOTE truth: learn/manim/scripts/binary-transparency.md

00:00.000 --> 00:12.000
Your operating system checks that an app is signed before it runs it. That check proves less than you'd think.

00:12.000 --> 00:24.000
A signature proves the company's key produced these bytes. It says nothing about what the bytes do. A backdoored build, signed by us, under pressure, passes every check your computer makes.

00:24.000 --> 00:35.000
So we publish the fingerprint of every desktop release, and every over-the-air mobile update, in public. The question changes. Not "did they sign it?" but "is this the app they gave everyone, or one made just for me?"

00:35.000 --> 00:50.000
Each release goes in at three layers, because "the app" isn't one file. The payload: the compiled program before any signature. That's the layer someone else can rebuild from source and compare.

00:50.000 --> 01:05.000
The signed layer: what you actually download, after notarization. Signing embeds unique data on purpose, so this can never be byte-identical twice. We log it separately, tied to its payload.

01:05.000 --> 01:15.000
And the third layer exists because of what a running app can actually measure.

01:15.000 --> 01:32.000
The in-app "verify this build" button needs something to compare the running app against. But payload is a fingerprint of a whole directory, or of an installer file. An app that's already installed has neither. It's just… itself.

01:32.000 --> 01:45.000
So an installed app can never match its payload fingerprint, even when it's genuine. The check needs the right thing to compare against.

01:45.000 --> 02:00.000
That's the third layer: the fingerprint of the main program exactly as installed, the one thing a running app can measure. And releases from before that layer have nothing to compare against, so the app says "can't check", not "you've been tampered with".

02:00.000 --> 02:20.000
Here's what it buys you. Two people download the app. One gets the public build, its fingerprint's in the log. One gets a special build, just for them.

02:20.000 --> 02:40.000
Either its fingerprint isn't in the log, and their own app notices, or we put it in the log, where it sits in public, permanently, next to the real release. There's no quiet option.

02:40.000 --> 02:55.000
Now the part to be careful about. A fingerprint proves we published these bytes. It does not prove they came from the source code you can read. For that you need reproducible builds, anyone compiles the source and gets byte-identical output.

02:55.000 --> 03:10.000
On Linux, we have that. A rebuild that uses none of our secrets, and that anyone can run from their own copy, rebuilds the AppImage and confirms the hash we logged.

03:10.000 --> 03:22.000
On macOS and Windows, the log proves you got the bytes we published, and those builds are not rebuilt from source. And the signing layer is never reproducible anywhere. That's by design: signing mixes in unique data.

03:22.000 --> 03:40.000
There's one more leg. Every release also carries a provenance record in a public log that isn't ours, proving which workflow produced the bytes, with no key of ours involved in checking it. A different question from reproducibility.
